  "body": "## What changed\n\nA new command converts standalone document tools to component stacks in one undo step. The tool library window shows a preview first. An MCP tool has a dry run that returns the same preview.\n\n- `crates/app/src/tool_convert.rs` (new): the plan. For each standalone tool it proposes a stack and gives a verdict:\n  - the stack is: the tool's library holder as the built-in holder component, then a collet that fits the holder and the shank (none for a Weldon or shrink-fit holder), then the tool itself as a solid cutting item `user:tool:<name>`;\n  - `same`: the stack derives the same tool record and gauge length;\n  - `different`: the stack derives other values, and the row lists each difference;\n  - `no match`: the row names the reason (no holder, no collet for this shank, a lathe tool, a stack that a `ChangeTool` law refuses).\n- `crates/app/src/commands/tool_convert.rs` (new): `ConvertToComponents`. It lands the selected rows in one undo step: the new table rows (built-in copies keep their library link), each stack, and each derived record and body. A `no match` row refuses the whole command by the tool name and the reason. Nothing changes then. `same_rows` selects every `same` row.\n- `crates/app/src/commands/tool_assembly.rs`: `ToolUpdate` is `pub(crate)`, so the new command uses the same per-tool update as `SetToolAssembly`.\n- `crates/app/src/mcp/convert_tools.rs` (new): `convert_tools_to_components { tools?, dry_run? }`. `dry_run: true` returns the preview rows and changes nothing. Without `tools`, only the `same` rows convert.\n- UI: \"Convert to components…\" in the tool list header opens the preview in place of the list (`tool_library_dialog/convert.rs`, `state_tool_convert.rs`). Each row has a tick, the tool, the proposed stack and the result. Only the `same` rows start ticked. A `no match` row cannot be ticked. \"Convert selected\" queues one `ConvertToComponents`. \"Select every row that converts\" also ticks the `different` rows. The preview is computed on open, on Refresh and after a convert, never on a still frame. If the history moves after the preview, \"Convert selected\" is disabled until Refresh.\n- An old library holder has the default stick-out: flute length plus shank length. Then no shank sits in the holder, and the stack resolver refuses it. For such a tool, the cutting item gets one shank diameter more of shank, and the gauge length stays the same. The row is `different` and names the shank length change. The decision file gives the reasons.\n- `docs/agents/mcp-tools.md` and the site MCP guide name the new tool.\n- `docs/decisions/4339-convert-to-components.md` records the decisions.\n\n## Pinning tests\n\nApp, `crates/app/tests/tool_library_holders/convert.rs` (binary `tool_library_holders`):\n- `convert::the_preview_names_each_verdict`\n- `convert::the_same_rows_convert_in_one_undo_step_and_undo_restores_every_tool`\n- `convert::a_different_row_converts_only_when_it_is_named`\n- `convert::a_no_match_row_refuses_the_whole_command_by_name`\n- `convert::the_posted_program_of_a_converted_tool_does_not_change`\n- `convert::mcp_dry_run_returns_the_preview_and_apply_converts_the_named_rows`\n\nUI, `crates/ui/src/egui_app/tests/tool_library_convert.rs`:\n- `egui_app::tests::tool_library_convert::the_preview_converts_the_ticked_rows_in_one_undo_step`\n\nUI `#[test]` count: 2007 before, 2008 after.\n\n## Gate\n\nQuick gate on head `8948a8371`, log `gate-4339-8948a83.log`: `cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/main` ran 393 steps, 0 failed, 0 stalled. The steps include `app suites: tool_library_holders`, `mcp_doc_drift` and the `camcraft-ui` lib tests. `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-ui --all-targets -- -D warnings` is clean in the same lanes run.\n\nThe first quick run (head `df2b6bc`) failed on two of the new tests. They expected the 6 mm collet, but the catalog end mill EM-0600-4FL has an 8 mm shank. Commit `8948a8371` corrects the expectations. The code did not change.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- A `.camtools` file is not converted directly. It converts after its import (import, convert, export). \"My library\" holds components, not tools.\n- Indexable tools convert as one solid cutting item. The tool record names no insert and no cutter body, so no split into insert and body is proposed.\n- Lathe tools are `no match` by design.\n- The light lane and the example catalog suites were not run. This PR changes no post and no toolpath generator. The posted program of a converted tool is pinned by `convert::the_posted_program_of_a_converted_tool_does_not_change`.\n- The `camcraft-mcp` skill has no row for the new tool yet. The skill is a process file. Follow-up: #4448.\n\n## Screenshots\n\nBefore (main): the tool list header has no conversion step.\n\n![before](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qsmits/Camcraft/pr-screenshots/4481/before.png)\n\nAfter: the header has \"Convert to components…\".\n\n![after-list](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qsmits/Camcraft/pr-screenshots/4481/after-list.png)\n\nAfter: the preview. Two rows are `same` and ticked. The old default stick-out row is `different` and names the shank length change. The rows with no holder are `no match` and cannot be ticked.\n\n![after-preview](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qsmits/Camcraft/pr-screenshots/4481/after-preview.png)\n\nNew top-level test files: none. The app tests join the `tool_library_holders` suite. The UI test is a new module of the `camcraft-ui` lib tests.\n\nChangelog: The tool library converts standalone tools to component stacks in one undo step, with a preview (same, different, no match) and an MCP tool `convert_tools_to_components` with a dry run.\nEffort: high\n\nFixes #4339\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
